t number of documents (40) and the highest average number of pages (192), indicating that financial reports are the most extensive documents in the benchmark.\n  - **News Documents**: The News category has the fewest documents (50) and the fewest average pages (1), suggesting that news articles are the shortest documents.\n  - **Academic and Legal Documents**: These categories have a moderate number of documents and questions, with average page counts of 11 and 58, respectively.\n- **Statistical Insights and Trends**:\n  - The distribution of documents across categories shows a wide range, from the most extensive financial reports to the concise news articles.\n  - The number of questions per document type varies significantly, with Academic and Legal documents having the highest number of questions.\n- **Relationships Between Data Elements**:\n  - There is a clear relationship between the number of documents and the number of questions, with categories that have more documents also generally having more questions.\n  - The average page count is directly related to the document type, with Financial documents being the longest and News documents the shortest.\n- **Significance of the Data Presented in Relation to Surrounding Context**:\n  - This table is significant as it provides a foundational understanding of the DocBench benchmark, which is used to evaluate the performance of multimodal retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems.\n  - The diversity in document types and their characteristics (length, number of questions) is crucial for testing the robustness and adaptability of RAG systems across different document types.\n- **How the Table Supports or Illustrates Concepts from the Surrounding Content**:\n  - The table supports the concept of evaluating RAG systems in diverse and complex document types, as highlighted in the surrounding content. It demonstrates the need for RAG systems to handle documents of varying lengths and complexities.\n  - The table also illustrates the importance of context-aware multimodal prompting, as discussed in the surrounding content, by showing the need to process different types of documents with varying numbers of questions and pages.\n\nEntity Info: {\n    "}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/f4207144427f6c162d05c828b9fe66a2adfe497515e2f8cd93db7f0427ec56ad.jpg\nCaption: Table 1: Statistics of Experimental Datasets.\nStructure: <table><tr><td>Dataset</td><td># Documents</td><td># Avg. Pages</td><td># Avg. Tokens</td><td># Doc Types</td><td># Questions</td></tr><tr><td>DocBench</td><td>229</td><td>66</td><td>46377</td><td>5</td><td>1102</td></tr><tr><td>MMLongBench</td><td>135</td><td>47.5</td><td>21214</td><td>7</td><td>1082</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table provides a detailed breakdown of the experimental datasets used in the evaluation of the RAG-Anything framework. The table is structured with columns for the dataset name, number of documents, average number of pages, average number of tokens, number of document types, and number of questions. The data points reveal that DocBench contains 229 documents, each averaging 66 pages and 46,377 tokens, across 5 document types, with 1,102 questions. MMLongBench, on the other hand, includes 135 documents, each averaging 47.5 pages and 21,214 tokens, across 7 document types, with 1,082 questions. These statistics highlight the complexity and diversity of the datasets, which are designed to test the framework's ability to handle long-context and multimodal documents effectively. The table supports the surrounding content by providing concrete data to back up the claims about the framework's performance and the challenges it addresses, such as long-context understanding and multimodal document comprehension."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/d462838e30a55f01869a9c8ede71175f3966021ff85384a14e9af8858bdb2202.jpg\nCaption: Table 2: Accuracy (%) on DocBench Dataset. Performance results with best scores highlighted in dark blue and second-best in light blue. Domain categories include Academia (Aca.), Finance (Fin.), Government (Gov.), Legal Documents (Law), and News Articles (News). Document types are categorized as Text-only (Txt.), Multimodal (Mm.), and Unanswerable queries (Una.).\nStructure: <table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Method</td><td colspan=\"5\">Domains</td><td colspan=\"3\">Types</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Overall</td></tr><tr><td>Aca.</td><td>Fin.</td><td>Gov.</td><td>Law.</td><td>News</td><td>Txt.</td><td>Mm.</td><td>Una.</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-4o-mini</td><td>40.3</td><td>46.9</td><td>60.3</td><td>59.2</td><td>61.0</td><td>61.0</td><td>43.8</td><td>49.6</td><td>51.2</td></tr><tr><td>LightRAG</td><td>53.8</td><td>56.2</td><td>59.5</td><td>61.8</td><td>65.7</td><td>85.0</td><td>59.7</td><td>46.8</td><td>58.4</td></tr><tr><td>MMGraphRAG</td><td>64.3</td><td>52.8</td><td>64.9</td><td>40.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>67.6</td><td>66.0</td><td>60.5</td><td>61.0</td></tr><tr><td>RAGAnything</td><td>61.4</td><td>67.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.2</td><td>66.3</td><td>85.0</td><td>76.3</td><td>46.0</td><td>63.4</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table provides a detailed comparison of the performance of different methods on the DocBench dataset. The table is structured with 'Method' as the first column, followed by columns for different domains (Academia, Finance, Government, Legal Documents, and News Articles) and document types (Text-only, Multimodal, and Unanswerable queries). The last column shows the overall performance. Key data points and patterns include:\n\n- **Overall Performance**: RAG-Anything outperforms other methods, achieving 63.4% overall accuracy, which is significantly higher than the 51.2% for GPT-4o-mini, 58.4% for LightRAG, and 61.0% for MMGraphRAG.\n- **Domain Performance**: RAG-Anything performs particularly well in the 'News' domain (66.3%) and 'Multimodal' document types (85.0%), while LightRAG excels in the 'Text-only' domain (85.0%).\n- **Trends**: The table highlights that RAG-Anything's performance improves significantly with longer documents, as seen in the '101-200 pages' and '200+ pages' categories on the MMLongBench dataset.\n\nThe table supports the claim that RAG-Anything's dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism are particularly effective for long-document reasoning tasks. The performance gap between RAG-Anything and other methods widens as document length increases, confirming the significance of its architectural design.\n\nThe table also indicates that LightRAG and MMGraphRAG have limitations, with LightRAG being restricted to text-only content and MMGraphRAG missing crucial structural information in tables and equations."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/839f92618d199653abe4d5ec90b8c2bdc89e67b9b8ca491f21605ad07ad5a18d.jpg\nCaption: Table 4: Ablation study results on DocBench. The “Chunk-only” variant bypasses dual-graph construction and relies solely on traditional chunk-based retrieval, while “w/o Reranker” eliminates cross-modal reranking but preserves the core graph-based architecture.\nStructure: <table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Method</td><td colspan=\"5\">Domains</td><td colspan=\"3\">Types</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Overall</td></tr><tr><td>Aca.</td><td>Fin.</td><td>Gov.</td><td>Law.</td><td>News</td><td>Txt.</td><td>Mm.</td><td>Una.</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk-only</td><td>55.8</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.1</td><td>60.7</td><td>64.0</td><td>81.6</td><td>66.2</td><td>43.5</td><td>60.0</td></tr><tr><td>w/o Reranker</td><td>60.9</td><td>63.5</td><td>58.8</td><td>60.2</td><td>68.6</td><td>81.7</td><td>74.7</td><td>45.4</td><td>62.4</td></tr><tr><td>RAGAnything</td><td>61.4</td><td>67.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.2</td><td>66.3</td><td>85.0</td><td>76.3</td><td>46.0</td><td>63.4</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table in question is Table 4 from the document, which presents ablation study results on the DocBench dataset. The table is structured with a header row and a header column, providing a clear and organized layout. The header row includes 'Method' and 'Domains' (Academic, Financial, Government, Law, News) and 'Types' (Text, Multimodal, Unimodal), while the 'Overall' column provides a summary of the performance across all domains and types. The rows below the header represent different methods: 'Chunk-only', 'w/o Reranker', and 'RAGAnything'. \n\nKey data points and patterns include:\n- The 'Chunk-only' method, which bypasses dual-graph construction, achieves lower accuracy across all domains and types, with the lowest overall accuracy of 60.0%.\n- The 'w/o Reranker' method, which retains the core graph-based architecture but removes the cross-modal reranking component, shows improved performance in some domains, particularly in the 'Multimodal' and 'Unimodal' types, with an overall accuracy of 62.4%.\n- The 'RAGAnything' method, which fully utilizes the dual-graph construction and hybrid retrieval architecture, demonstrates the highest overall accuracy of 63.4%, with significant improvements in the 'Multimodal' and 'Unimodal' types.\n\nStatistical insights and trends indicate that the dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism are crucial for handling long-context documents and complex multimodal data. The performance gap between 'Chunk-only' and 'RAGAnything' is particularly pronounced for longer documents, confirming the effectiveness of the proposed architecture.\n\nRelationships between data elements are evident in the structured representation of the methods and their performance across different domains and types. The 'RAGAnything' method's superior performance across all categories underscores the importance of preserving structural relationships and leveraging multimodal information.\n\nThe significance of this table in the context of the surrounding content is that it provides empirical evidence supporting the claims made in the document. Specifically, it demonstrates that the RAG-Anything framework, which integrates dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval, outperforms traditional chunk-based retrieval methods and other baselines, especially in long-context and multimodal document scenarios. This aligns with the document's emphasis on the importance of structured context and multimodal information for accurate document reasoning tasks.\n\nThe table supports the concepts from the surrounding content by illustrating the effectiveness of the RAG-Anything framework in handling complex, multimodal documents, which is crucial for tasks such as long-context reasoning and information extraction from financial reports and research papers."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/4dafc32b233a45f226a4c12e429034cb9e37529272541e77f5d0c7be2888ff0b.jpg\nCaption: Table 3: Accuracy (%) on MMLongBench across different domains and overall performance. Best results are highlighted in dark blue and second-best in light blue.. Domain categories include Research Reports/Introductions (Res.), Tutorials/Workshops (Tut.), Academic Papers (Acad.), Guidebooks (Guid.), Brochures (Broch.), Administration/Industry Files (Admin.), and Financial Reports (Fin.).\nStructure: <table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Method</td><td colspan=\"7\">Domains</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Overall</td></tr><tr><td>Res.</td><td>Tut.</td><td>Acad.</td><td>Guid.</td><td>Broch.</td><td>Admin.</td><td>Fin.</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-4o-mini</td><td>35.5</td><td>44.0</td><td>24.6</td><td>33.1</td><td>29.5</td><td>46.8</td><td>31.1</td><td>33.5</td></tr><tr><td>LightRAG</td><td>40.8</td><td>34.1</td><td>36.2</td><td>39.4</td><td>41.0</td><td>44.4</td><td>38.3</td><td>38.9</td></tr><tr><td>MMGraphRAG</td><td>40.8</td><td>36.5</td><td>35.7</td><td>35.8</td><td>28.2</td><td>46.9</td><td>38.5</td><td>37.7</td></tr><tr><td>RAGAnything</td><td>46.6</td><td>43.5</td><td>38.7</td><td>43.9</td><td>34.0</td><td>45.7</td><td>43.6</td><td>42.8</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table provides a detailed comparison of the accuracy of different methods on the MMLongBench dataset, highlighting the performance of RAG-Anything. The table is structured with 'Method' as the first column and 'Domains' (Research Reports/Introductions, Tutorials/Workshops, Academic Papers, Guidebooks, Brochures, Administration/Industry Files, Financial Reports) and 'Overall' as the subsequent columns. The data points represent the accuracy (%) of each method across different domains and overall performance.\n\n- **Table Structure and Organization**: The table is well-organized, with clear headers and a consistent layout. The 'Method' column lists the different models being compared, and the 'Domains' and 'Overall' columns provide the accuracy scores for each method.\n- **Column Headers and Their Meanings**:\n  - **Method**: The name of the model or method being evaluated.\n  - **Domains**: The specific categories of documents (Research Reports/Introductions, Tutorials/Workshops, etc.) on which the accuracy is measured.\n  - **Overall**: The overall accuracy score across all domains.\n- **Key Data Points and Patterns**:\n  - **GPT-4o-mini**: Performs poorly across most domains, with the best performance in 'Tutorials/Workshops' at 44.0% and the worst in 'Academic Papers' at 24.6%.\n  - **LightRAG**: Shows moderate performance, with the best accuracy in 'Research Reports/Introductions' at 40.8% and the worst in 'Guidebooks' at 35.8%.\n  - **MMGraphRAG**: Performs similarly to LightRAG, with the best accuracy in 'Research Reports/Introductions' at 40.8% and the worst in 'Guidebooks' at 28.2%.\n  - **RAG-Anything**: Demonstrates superior performance across all domains, with the best accuracy in 'Research Reports/Introductions' at 46.6% and the worst in 'Guidebooks' at 34.0%. The overall accuracy is 42.8%, which is the highest among all methods.\n- **Statistical Insights and Trends**:\n  - RAG-Anything outperforms all other methods in most domains, particularly in 'Research Reports/Introductions' and 'Financial Reports'.\n  - The overall accuracy of RAG-Anything is significantly higher than the other methods, indicating its effectiveness in handling long-context documents.\n- **Relationships Between Data Elements**:\n  - The accuracy scores for each method vary across different domains, reflecting the complexity and diversity of the documents.\n  - RAG-Anything's superior performance can be attributed to its dual-graph construction and cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism, which effectively captures structural and cross-modal relationships.\n- **Significance of the Data Presented in Relation to Surrounding Context**:\n  - The table supports the claim that RAG-Anything is a superior method for long-context multimodal document understanding, as evidenced by its high accuracy scores across various domains.\n  - The table also highlights the limitations of other methods, such as GPT-4o-mini, which struggles with long documents, and LightRAG and MMGraphRAG, which are less effective in handling complex multimodal documents.\n- **How the Table Supports or Illustrates Concepts from the Surrounding Content**:\n  - The table directly illustrates the performance comparison between RAG-Anything and other methods, validating the claims made in the surrounding content about RAG-Anything's effectiveness in handling long-context documents and multimodal information."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 2.2 UNIVERSAL REPRESENTATION FOR HETEROGENEOUS KNOWLEDGE\n- Neighbor Text: Formally, each knowledge source $k _ { i } \\in \\mathcal { K } \\left( \\mathbf { e . g . , a } \\right.$ web page) is decomposed into atomic content units: where each unit $c _ { j }$ consists of a modality type $t _ { j } ~ \\in \\mathrm { { \\ t e x t } }$, image, table, equation, $\\dots$ and its corresponding raw content $x _ { j }$. The content $x _ { j }$ represents the extracted information from the original knowledge source, processed in a modality-aware manner to preserve semantic integrity. To ensure high-fidelity extraction, RAG-Anything leverages specialized parsers for different content types. Text is segmented into coherent paragraphs or list items. Figures are extracted with associated metadata such as captions and cross-references. Tables are parsed into structured cells with headers and values. Mathematical expressions are converted into symbolic representations. The resulting x<sub>j</sub> preserves both content and structural context within the source. This provides a faithful, modalityconsistent representation. The decomposition abstracts diverse file formats into atomic units while maintaining their hierarchical order and contextual relationships. This canonicalization enables uniform processing, indexing, and retrieval of multimodal content within our framework. 2.2.1 DUAL-GRAPH CONSTRUCTION FOR MULTIMODAL KNOWLEDGE While multimodal knowledge unification provides a uniform abstraction across modalities, directly constructing a single unified graph often risks overlooking modality-specific structural signals. The proposed RAG-Anything addresses this challenge through a dual-graph construction strategy. The system first builds a cross-modal knowledge graph that faithfully grounds non-textual modalities within their contextual environment. It then constructs a text-based knowledge graph using established text-centric extraction pipelines. These complementary graphs are merged through entity alignment. This design ensures accurate cross-modal grounding and comprehensive coverage of textual semantics, enabling richer knowledge representation and robust retrieval.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/d960511e87edf887a6ee67caba7d4f7cb9da80f3fe7cdea264ab856390831d14.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 1: Overview of our proposed universal RAG framework RAG-Anything.\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image presents a comprehensive architectural diagram of the 'RAG-Anything' framework, a universal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system. The diagram is organized horizontally into four main stages, flowing from left to right, illustrating the process from input to response generation.\n\n1.  **Input Documents (Far Left):** A vertical column labeled 'Input Documents' displays icons representing various file formats: PPT, PDF, DOC, JPG, PNG, XLS, and PPT. These feed into a 'Profile Parser' block.\n\n2.  **Multimodal Knowledge Unification (Left-Center):** This section is enclosed in a dashed box. It processes the parsed input into a 'Structured Content List'. The process involves four parallel extraction paths: 'Hierarchical Text Extraction' (yielding 'Text Info'), 'Image Caption & Metadata Extraction' (yielding 'Image Info'), 'LaTeX Equation Recognition' (yielding 'Equation Info'), and 'Table Structure & Content Parsing' (yielding 'Table Info'). These outputs are represented as vertical stacks of colored boxes (white, light blue, light purple, light green).\n\n3.  **Dual-Graph Construction for Multimodal Knowledge (Center):** This central section, also in a dashed box, is subtitled '(each document)'. It splits into two parallel processing streams:\n    *   **Text-Based Knowledge Graph:** The 'Text Info' stream goes through 'Text Info Processing' and 'Entity & Relation Extraction' (indicated by icons of a brain and a network). This produces a small graph with nodes labeled 'Developer', 'Bee', and 'Observe'.\n    *   **Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph:** The 'Image Info', 'Equation Info', and 'Table Info' streams are processed by 'Multi-modal Processors' and 'VLM/LLM' to generate 'Textual Multi-modal Info'. This leads to a 'Cross-Modal Knowledge Graph' with nodes labeled 'Parent Node: Multi-modal Instance' and 'Child Node'.\n    *   **Merging:** Both graphs are 'Merged' into a larger 'Knowledge Graph' (labeled 'KG over All Documents') containing 'Merged Node' structures. Simultaneously, a 'Text Encoder' processes the textual info to create a 'Vector Database' (labeled 'VDB over All Documents'), which merges 'Text VDB' and 'Multi-modal VDB'.\n\n4.  **Retrieval and Response (Right):** The final stage begins with a 'Query' icon and a sample question in a speech bubble: 'Could you share insights on the experimental results and data tables?'. The query undergoes 'Query High-/Low-level Keys Extraction' and 'Text Encoder' processing. This leads to 'Structural Knowledge Navigation' (using the KG) and 'Semantic Similarity Matching' (using the VDB). These steps produce 'Hybrid Retrieved Info', which is fed into an 'LLM' to generate the final 'Response'. A sample response is shown in a speech bubble: 'Based on the experimental data, the results revealed...'.\n\nThe diagram uses a clean, schematic style with dashed lines for process boundaries, solid arrows for data flow, and distinct colors to differentiate modalities (e.g., blue for text, purple for equations, green for tables)."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > A.3 CONTEXT-AWARE MULTIMODAL PROMPTING\n- Neighbor Text: Table Analysis Prompt. Figure 8 structures systematic tabular content decomposition across multiple analytical dimensions: structural organization, column semantics, critical values, statistical patterns, and contextual relevance. Through precise terminology and numerical accuracy requirements, the prompt eliminates ambiguous generalizations and ensures faithful preservation of key indicators while maintaining coherent alignment with surrounding discourse. Equation Analysis Prompt. Figure 9 prioritizes semantic interpretation over syntactic restatement of mathematical expressions. The prompt instructs comprehensive analysis of variable definitions, operational logic, theoretical foundations, inter-formula relationships, and practical applications. This methodology ensures mathematical content becomes integral to broader argumentative frameworks, supporting enhanced retrieval accuracy, analytical traceability, and reasoning coherence. Figure 10 presents the standardized prompt specifically designed for systematic factual accuracy assessment of generated responses across multiple domains. The prompt establishes explicit evaluation criteria that prioritize content correctness over stylistic considerations, producing binary accuracy classifications accompanied by concise analytical justifications. All accuracy evaluations throughout our comprehensive experimental framework were conducted using GPT-4o-mini, ensuring consistent and reliable assessment standards across diverse question categories and specialized domains.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/3274479bdaa616f49db4c299fc1f838f4463cb066ef8e36b050055bb30e9b150.jpg\nCaptions: Figure 7: Vision analysis prompt